---
import JournalFigure from '../components/figures/JournalFigure.astro';
import ResearchFigure from '../components/figures/ResearchFigure.astro';
import Pathway from '../components/figures/Pathway.astro';
import StudyAnatomy from '../components/figures/StudyAnatomy.astro';
import TeamFigure from '../components/figures/TeamFigure.astro';
import ArticleEnd from '../components/journal/ArticleEnd.astro';
import ArticleHead from '../components/journal/ArticleHead.astro';
import Cite from '../components/journal/Cite.astro';
import JSection from '../components/journal/JSection.astro';
import NextSteps from '../components/journal/NextSteps.astro';
import Registration from '../components/journal/Registration.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
import { accArticle, contact, nrmp, social } from '../data/site';

const sections = [
	{ id: 'introduction', label: 'Why Research Matters' },
	{ id: 'methods', label: 'How RC Works' },
	{ id: 'results', label: 'What Students Achieved' },
	{ id: 'discussion', label: 'Why It Works' },
];

// The three questions a newcomer arrives with, answered in a sentence each.
const summary = [
	{
		q: 'What is RC?',
		a: 'An online school that teaches medical students and graduates in any country to do clinical research, then supervises their first studies until they are submitted to journals.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How does it work?',
		a: 'You take Level 1, a 2-day introduction, then a course in one research method. Then you join a small project group, where a mentor guides you through a real study.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Does it work?',
		a: 'By 2024, more than 3,500 students had trained with RC and published more than 600 papers. In Match Mentorship, 90% of students matched in 2025 and 80% in 2026.',
	},
];

const refs = [
	{ text: 'National Resident Matching Program. Charting Outcomes: characteristics of applicants who matched to their preferred specialty. Non-U.S. IMGs, 2026 Main Residency Match. July 2026.', href: nrmp },
	{ text: 'United States Medical Licensing Examination. USMLE Step 1 transition to pass/fail only score reporting. January 2022.', href: 'https://www.usmle.org/usmle-step-1-transition-passfail-only-score-reporting' },
	{ text: 'National Resident Matching Program. Charting Outcomes in the Match: international medical graduates. 2020, 2022 and 2024 Main Residency Match, Table 2.', href: 'https://www.nrmp.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/Charting_Outcomes_IMG2024.pdf' },
	{ text: 'National Resident Matching Program. Charting Outcomes: Program Director Survey Results, 2026 Main Residency Match. Narrative summary. August 2026.', href: 'https://www.nrmp.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-PD-Survey-Report-Narrative-FINAL.pdf' },
	{ text: 'Khan MS. Fostering the next generation of physician-scientists in developing countries. American College of Cardiology, Fellows in Training Section. January 22, 2024.', href: accArticle },
	{ text: 'Research Consultancy. The year in review, 2025. December 2025.', href: 'https://www.facebook.com/researchconsultancyrc' },
	{ text: 'Research Consultancy. Match 2025 and Match 2026 results. March 2026.', href: 'https://www.facebook.com/researchconsultancyrc' },
];
---

<Layout
	accent="rust"
	runningHead="Your Name et al."
	jsonLd={{
		'@type': 'EducationalOrganization',
		name: 'Research Consultancy',
		url: Astro.site?.href,
		logo: Astro.site ? new URL('/rc-mark.png', Astro.site).href : undefined,
		email: contact.email,
		foundingDate: '2016',
		sameAs: [social.instagram, social.facebook, social.linkedin],
	}}
	description="Research Consultancy teaches medical students in any country to do clinical research, then supervises their first studies until they are submitted. Start with Level 1: two days, live on Zoom.">
	<ArticleHead
		type="Original Article"
		page="e001"
		title="From First Course to First Paper: How Research Consultancy Trains Medical Students"
		summary={summary}
		columns
		fork={{ lead: 'Applying for residency this cycle?', label: 'Match Mentorship is built for you', href: '/match-mentorship' }}
		sections={sections}
	>
		<div slot="byline" class="mt-5">
			<p class="j-byline text-ink-secondary">
				<span class="relative inline-block font-semibold text-ink">
					<span class="marker absolute inset-x-[-4px] bottom-[0.06em] h-[0.45em] rounded-[2px] bg-orange/60"></span>
					<span class="relative">Your Name<sup>1</sup></span>
				</span>; M. Shahzeb Khan, MD, MS<sup>2</sup>; Kaneez Fatima, MD<sup>2</sup>; M. Shariq Usman, MD<sup>3</sup>; Tariq Jamal Siddiqi, MD<sup>2</sup>
			</p>
			<p class="j-affil mt-1"><sup>1</sup>Your medical school, anywhere in the world. <sup>2</sup>Baylor Scott &amp; White, Dallas, Texas. <sup>3</sup>UT Southwestern Medical Center, Dallas, Texas.</p>
			<p class="you-note mt-3 flex items-start gap-2 text-[14px] leading-snug text-[#b3480f]">
				<svg viewBox="0 0 28 22" class="mt-[-0.35rem] h-5 w-7 shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 19C12 19 5 13 5 3" /><path d="M1.5 6.5 5 2.5l3.5 4" /></svg>
				<span><span class="font-semibold">Yes, you.</span> RC students are named as authors on the papers they write, with their faculty beside them.</span>
			</p>
		</div>
		<div slot="action" data-action-panel class="grid gap-4 border-y border-[#bdb9b3] py-4 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] sm:items-center sm:gap-8">
			<div>
				<p class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-[0.08em] text-navy uppercase">
					<a href={withBase('/courses/level-1')} class="underline decoration-orange/50 underline-offset-[3px] hover:decoration-orange">Level 1</a>, the first course
				</p>
				<Registration slug="level-1" variant="row" class="reg-strip mt-1.5" />
			</div>
			<a href={contact.whatsapp.href} class="btn-secondary self-start sm:self-center" rel="noopener">
				<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-4 text-green" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2a10 10 0 0 0-8.6 15.1L2 22l5-1.3A10 10 0 1 0 12 2Zm0 18.2c-1.5 0-3-.4-4.2-1.2l-.3-.2-3 .8.8-2.9-.2-.3A8.2 8.2 0 1 1 12 20.2Zm4.5-6.1c-.2-.1-1.5-.7-1.7-.8-.2-.1-.4-.1-.6.1l-.8 1c-.1.2-.3.2-.5.1a6.7 6.7 0 0 1-3.3-2.9c-.3-.4.3-.4.8-1.4.1-.2 0-.3 0-.4l-.8-1.8c-.2-.5-.4-.4-.6-.4h-.5a1 1 0 0 0-.7.3 3 3 0 0 0-.9 2.2 5.2 5.2 0 0 0 1.1 2.7 11.8 11.8 0 0 0 4.5 4c1.7.7 2.3.8 3.2.6.5-.1 1.5-.6 1.7-1.2.2-.6.2-1.1.2-1.2-.1-.1-.3-.2-.5-.3Z"/></svg>
				Ask us on WhatsApp
			</a>
		</div>
		<Pathway slot="illustration" class="ring-1 ring-hairline" />
	</ArticleHead>

	<JSection title="Why Research Matters for the Match" label="Introduction" id="introduction">
		<div class="j-main j-prose">
			<p class="drop-cap">
				The Match is hardest for international graduates. In 2026, 41.5% of graduates of medical schools outside the United
				States matched in the specialty they ranked first.<Cite n={1} /> Since Step 1 became pass/fail in 2022,<Cite n={2} />{' '}
				programs have one less number to sort applicants by, and research has become one of the clearest ways to stand out.
			</p>
			<p>
				NRMP's own data show it. Before the change, international graduates who matched reported slightly less research than
				those who did not; since Step 1 went pass/fail, those who matched have reported more, and their output keeps
				rising (Figure 1A).<Cite n={3} /> In most specialties, matched applicants had published half as much again or twice
				as much as those who did not match: in pathology a median of 7 papers against 4, and in radiation oncology 19
				against 3 (Figure 1B).<Cite n={1} />
			</p>
			<p>
				A research project with U.S. faculty in your specialty brings more than papers. It brings a letter from someone who
				has seen your work, and letters from the specialty are among the three things program directors cite most when
				choosing whom to interview.<Cite n={4} />
			</p>
		</div>
		<ResearchFigure class="j-span" label="Figure 1" cite={{ trend: 3, specialty: 1 }} />

		<div class="j-main j-prose">
			<h3 class="j-subhead">What a Study Is</h3>
			<p>
				A clinical study asks a question about patients, finds data that can answer it and analyzes them. The result is
				presented as an abstract at a scientific meeting, then written up as a paper that other doctors review before a
				journal publishes it (Figure 2A).
			</p>
			<p>
				Few medical schools outside the United States teach these steps, and students who want to learn seldom find a
				mentor.<Cite n={5} /> RC was founded in 2016 to teach them.
			</p>
		</div>
		<StudyAnatomy class="j-span" label="Figure 2" wide />
	</JSection>

	<JSection title="How RC Works" label="Methods" id="methods" newPage>
		<div class="j-main j-prose">
			<h3 class="j-subhead">Level 1</h3>
			<p>
				Every student starts with Level 1: 2 days of live teaching on Zoom in study design, scientific writing and
				statistics. It assumes no experience.
			</p>
			<h3 class="j-subhead">A Method Course</h3>
			<p>
				Next, students choose one of 4 courses in a method they can use from any country. A meta-analysis pools the results
				of published trials; the other 3 courses teach studies of U.S. national health databases. None needs patients or
				hospital access (Figure 2B). <a href={withBase('/courses')}>Compare the courses</a>.
			</p>
			<h3 class="j-subhead">A Mentored Project</h3>
			<p>
				When the course ends, each student joins a small project group. A mentor, usually a former RC student, leads the
				group and reads every draft, research analysts check the statistics, and faculty at U.S. hospitals choose the
				questions and check the methods (Figure 3).
				Students run the analysis, write the paper and are named as authors for the work they did.
			</p>
		</div>
		<TeamFigure class="j-margin" label="Figure 3" />
	</JSection>

	<JSection title="What Students Have Achieved" label="Results" id="results" newPage>
		<div class="j-main j-prose">
			<p>
				By 2024, more than 3,500 students had trained with RC and published more than 600 papers.<Cite n={5} /> In 2025
				alone, students published more than 150 papers, in journals including <em>The Lancet</em>, <em>JAMA</em> and{' '}
				<em>Circulation</em>, and presented 40 abstracts at the American College of Cardiology meeting.<Cite n={6} />
			</p>
			<p>
				They publish in their own specialties, not only in cardiology (Figure 4). Four students' paths from their first
				course, with dates, are in the <a href={withBase('/results#careers')}>Supplemental Material</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				In Match Mentorship, RC's 12-month program for residency applicants, 90% of students matched in 2025 and 80% in
				2026.<Cite n={7} /> More than 20 others have held research posts at U.S. hospitals, a common step before
				applying.<Cite n={5} /> <a href={withBase('/results')}>See every result</a>.
			</p>
		</div>
		<JournalFigure class="j-margin" label="Figure 4" />
	</JSection>

	<JSection title="Why It Works" label="Discussion" id="discussion" newPage>
		<div class="j-main j-prose">
			<p>
				Three things explain these results. The methods need no patients, laboratory or ethics committee, so a student can
				start from any country. Supervision continues past the course, until the paper is submitted. And the supervisors
				have done the same work recently: mentors took the same courses a year or two earlier, and RC's 4 faculty, with
				more than 900 publications between them, publish in the journals students submit to.{' '}
				<a href={withBase('/mentors')}>Meet the faculty, research analysts and mentors</a>.
			</p>
			<h3 class="j-subhead">Conclusions</h3>
			<p>
				Students with no research experience can learn to publish before they apply for residency, when teaching is
				followed by supervised work on real studies. The first step is Level 1.
			</p>
		</div>
		<aside class="j-margin j-box self-start p-5">
			<h3 class="j-box-title text-[15px]">Limitations</h3>
			<ul class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-[14.5px] leading-snug text-ink-secondary">
				<li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.5em] size-1.5 shrink-0 bg-navy"></span>No program can guarantee a publication or a match. Be wary of any that does.</li>
				<li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.5em] size-1.5 shrink-0 bg-navy"></span>Authorship depends on each student's contribution.</li>
				<li class="flex gap-2.5"><span class="mt-[0.5em] size-1.5 shrink-0 bg-navy"></span>These results describe students who chose RC and stayed. There is no comparison group.</li>
			</ul>
		</aside>
	</JSection>

	<NextSteps />

	<ArticleEnd page="e001" refs={refs} supplement />
</Layout>

<style>
	/* Your name is highlighted on the byline once the page settles. */
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		.marker {
			transform-origin: left;
			animation: mark 700ms cubic-bezier(0.3, 0.7, 0.2, 1) 1.2s both;
		}
		@keyframes mark {
			from {
				transform: scaleX(0);
			}
			to {
				transform: scaleX(1);
			}
		}
	}
</style>
